from common import lesson, lab, cite, run, code

LESSONS = [
lesson(11,3,'modules-and-state','nn.Module, parameters, state_dict, and reading a module tree',
 'A model is a tree of modules, each with named parameters and a forward method. Learn to read the tree and the rest of the toolkit becomes navigation.',
 '''<h2 id="what-a-module-is">What a module is</h2>
<p><code>nn.Module</code> is a class with three jobs: it owns parameters, it owns child modules, and it defines <code>forward</code>. Assigning an <code>nn.Linear</code> to <code>self.up</code> inside <code>__init__</code> registers it as a child; the parent then finds the child's parameters through <code>parameters()</code>, saves them through <code>state_dict()</code>, and moves them with <code>.to(device)</code>. Calling the module, <code>mlp(x)</code>, runs <code>forward</code> plus the bookkeeping that fires hooks, which is why you call the module and never <code>forward</code> directly.</p>
'''+run('python3 labs/examples/module_tree.py')+'''
<h2 id="tiny-mlp">Read the tiny MLP</h2>
<p>Printing a module prints its tree, with each leaf's constructor arguments. <code>named_parameters()</code> yields <code>(name, Parameter)</code> pairs '''+cite('torch-module')+''', and the names are dotted paths through the tree: <code>up.weight</code>, <code>down.bias</code>. The shapes carry a convention worth memorising: <code>nn.Linear(8, 32).weight</code> is <code>(32, 8)</code>, stored as <code>(out_features, in_features)</code>, so the forward computes <code>x @ W.T + b</code>. GPT-2's <code>Conv1D</code> layers below use the opposite convention, <code>(in, out)</code>, which is a historical quirk you will meet when reading its weights in the transformer course.</p>
<p><code>state_dict()</code> returns a dictionary from those same names to tensors, containing parameters and persistent buffers; the documentation notes it is a shallow copy holding references to the module's tensors '''+cite('torch-module')+''', so editing a value in it edits the model. Saving a model is saving this dict; loading is <code>load_state_dict</code>. 552 parameters for the tiny MLP is <code>8·32 + 32 + 32·8 + 8</code>; count it once by hand so you trust <code>numel()</code>.</p>
<h2 id="gpt2-tree">Read GPT-2 small's tree</h2>
<p>The top level has two children: <code>transformer</code>, the stack, and <code>lm_head</code>, the unembedding. Inside <code>transformer</code>: <code>wte</code>, the token embedding of shape <code>(50257, 768)</code>; <code>wpe</code>, the position embedding, <code>(1024, 768)</code>, which is why the context limit is 1024; <code>drop</code>, a dropout that does nothing in eval mode but is a convenient place to hook the embedding output; <code>h</code>, a <code>ModuleList</code> of 12 <code>GPT2Block</code>s; and <code>ln_f</code>, the final LayerNorm. Each block is <code>ln_1</code>, <code>attn</code>, <code>ln_2</code>, <code>mlp</code>: normalise, attend, normalise, feed forward, with the residual additions in the block's <code>forward</code>.</p>
<p>The parameter count, 124,439,808, is the "124M" in the model's name. The <code>lm_head.weight</code> shares storage with <code>wte.weight</code>: the same <code>data_ptr()</code>, as the output shows. Tied embeddings mean the <code>state_dict</code> has 149 entries while <code>named_parameters()</code> lists one tensor fewer by default, because it removes duplicates. <code>model.training</code> is <code>False</code> after <code>.eval()</code>; forgetting that call leaves dropout active and makes two forward passes disagree.</p>
<p><code>get_submodule("transformer.h.5.mlp")</code> walks a dotted path to a module. That string is the address you give to a hook, and it is the same path that appears in <code>state_dict</code> keys with <code>.weight</code> appended. TransformerLens renames these addresses (<code>blocks.5.mlp</code>) and nnsight keeps them; lesson 14 has the table.</p>
<h2 id="navigation">Navigating any model</h2>
<p>Four calls answer most questions about an unfamiliar model. <code>print(model)</code> for the tree. <code>[n for n, _ in model.named_children()]</code> for one level. <code>{n: tuple(p.shape) for n, p in model.named_parameters()}</code> for every shape. <code>model.config</code> for the hyperparameters a Hugging Face model was built from; <code>n_layer</code>, <code>n_embd</code> and <code>n_head</code> for GPT-2 are 12, 768 and 12 '''+cite('hf-gpt2')+'''. The sum of parameter sizes should match the published count, and if it does not you have the wrong checkpoint.</p>
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In eval and interpretability work</span><p>"Layer 5" is ambiguous until you say which module: <code>transformer.h.5</code> is the block, <code>transformer.h.5.mlp</code> its MLP, and <code>transformer.h.5.attn.c_proj</code> the attention output projection. Write the dotted path in your notes, not the number.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>Loading a model and not calling <code>.eval()</code>. Dropout stays on, activations change between runs, and a cache you compare against a later run differs by noise you then try to explain.</p></div>''',
 'Someone says GPT-2 small has "about 85M non-embedding parameters". From the tree above, how would you check that in two lines?',
 'Sum <code>p.numel()</code> over <code>named_parameters()</code> whose name does not start with <code>transformer.wte</code> or <code>transformer.wpe</code>. The embeddings are 50257·768 plus 1024·768, about 39.4M, so the remainder of 124.4M is about 85M. The check is a comprehension over names, which is why names matter.',
 lab('Write a function <code>shapes_by_block(model, i)</code> that returns a dict of every parameter name under <code>transformer.h.i</code> to its shape, and call it for block 0. Then confirm that every block has identical shapes.',
 'Filter <code>named_parameters()</code> by prefix <code>f"transformer.h.{i}."</code> and strip the prefix. Twelve identical dicts; <code>c_attn.weight</code> is <code>(768, 2304)</code>, three times 768 because queries, keys and values are produced by one matrix and split. That split is a reshape you will write in the transformer course.',
 ['python3 labs/examples/module_tree.py']), ['torch-module','hf-gpt2'], 'nn.Module parameters state_dict named_parameters module tree GPT2 config eval'),

lesson(12,3,'gpt2-forward','Loading GPT-2 small: tokenizer, input ids, logits, next token, hidden states',
 'One forward pass, read by shape at every step: text to ids, ids to logits, logits to a prediction, and the thirteen hidden states in between.',
 '''<h2 id="load">Load once, in eval mode</h2>
<p><code>AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained("gpt2")</code> and <code>AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained("gpt2")</code> read the cached files. The model is a <code>GPT2LMHeadModel</code>, the tree from lesson 11. Call <code>.eval()</code>. Loading takes a few seconds and should happen once per script or once per test class, never per prompt.</p>
'''+run('python3 labs/examples/gpt2_next_token.py')+'''
<h2 id="tokens">Text becomes ids of shape (batch, seq)</h2>
<p>The tokenizer returns a dict with <code>input_ids</code> and <code>attention_mask</code>; <code>return_tensors="pt"</code> makes them torch tensors with a batch axis of 1. Five tokens for five words here, but the tokens are <code>'The', 'Ġcapital', ...</code>: GPT-2's byte-pair encoding attaches the leading space to the word, and the <code>Ġ</code> is how it displays that space. A word you did not expect to split will split, and a space before the prompt changes the first token. Token counts are not word counts, and the <code>seq</code> axis counts tokens.</p>
<h2 id="logits">Logits are (batch, seq, vocab)</h2>
<p>The model returns a <code>ModelOutput</code> dataclass; <code>out.logits</code> has shape <code>(batch_size, sequence_length, config.vocab_size)</code>, the scores for every vocabulary item at every position before a softmax '''+cite('hf-gpt2')+'''. Position <code>i</code> holds the prediction for the token <em>after</em> position <code>i</code>, so the prediction for what follows the prompt is <code>out.logits[0, -1]</code>, shape <code>(50257,)</code>. <code>softmax(-1)</code> turns it into probabilities and <code>topk</code> reads off the best five: <code>' the'</code> at 8.5%, with <code>' Paris'</code> fifth at 3.2%. GPT-2 small is a weak model; that is fine, and it is also why the sentence is a useful test prompt: the right answer is present but not dominant, so an intervention that changes it is visible.</p>
<h2 id="hidden-states">Thirteen hidden states of (batch, seq, 768)</h2>
<p><code>output_hidden_states=True</code> adds <code>out.hidden_states</code>: a tuple with one tensor for the output of the embeddings plus one for the output of each layer, each of shape <code>(batch_size, sequence_length, hidden_size)</code> '''+cite('hf-outputs')+'''. Twelve layers plus the embedding is thirteen, and <code>hidden_size</code> is 768. The indexing convention is the one to get right once: <code>hidden_states[0]</code> is the embedding output, before any block; <code>hidden_states[i + 1]</code> is the residual stream after block <code>i</code>; so "after block 5" is index 6.</p>
<p>The last entry has a wrinkle. The documentation warns that <code>hidden_states[-1]</code> need not equal the last hidden state exactly because some models apply normalisation to it '''+cite('hf-outputs')+''', and GPT-2 is one of them: <code>hidden_states[12]</code> is the output of <code>ln_f</code>, not the raw output of block 11. Lesson 13 shows the difference directly with a hook. If you want the residual stream after the last block, hook the block.</p>
<p>The norms across layers grow from about 5 at the embedding to several hundred by the last block, then drop after <code>ln_f</code>. That growth is a known feature of GPT-2's residual stream and is why comparisons across layers normalise first; the linear-algebra course takes this up.</p>
<h2 id="batching">Batching needs a pad token and a mask</h2>
<p>GPT-2 ships with no pad token, as the <code>None</code> shows. Assigning <code>tokenizer.pad_token = tokenizer.eos_token</code> is the standard fix. A batch of two prompts of different lengths is padded to the longer, here on the right, and the <code>attention_mask</code> marks real tokens with 1 and padding with 0. Passing the mask makes attention ignore the padding; the output confirms the first prompt's activations are unchanged by batching to <code>1e-4</code>. The padded positions of the shorter prompt still produce numbers, and they are garbage: every reduction over the sequence axis must use the mask, which is what <code>masked_mean_pool</code> in the shape lab is for.</p>
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In eval and interpretability work</span><p>A next-token eval is <code>logits[:, -1].argmax(-1)</code> compared with a reference id, for a batch. Everything that can go wrong is a shape or tokenisation question: did you take the last real token or the last padded one, and is the reference a single token in this tokenizer.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>Off by one on layers. "Layer 6 activations" might mean <code>hidden_states[6]</code> (after block 5) or the output of block 6 (<code>hidden_states[7]</code>). State the block index and the convention in every file name and manifest; the readiness review asks for it.</p></div>''',
 'You batch ten prompts, pad on the right, and compute <code>out.logits[:, -1]</code> to score each prompt\'s next token. Which prompts are scored correctly?',
 'Only the longest ones. For a shorter prompt, position <code>-1</code> is a padding token and its logits predict what follows padding. Either pad on the left so the last real token is at <code>-1</code>, or index each row at <code>attention_mask.sum(-1) - 1</code>. The mask tells you where the real end is; use it.',
 lab('Change the prompt in a copy of <code>gpt2_next_token.py</code> to <code>"The capital of France is Paris. The capital of Germany is"</code> and read the top five. Then prepend a space to the prompt and compare the token list.',
 'The context makes <code>" Berlin"</code> likely; GPT-2 small does in-context pattern completion even at 124M. The leading space changes the first token from <code>The</code> to <code>ĠThe</code>, a different id, which is why prompt formatting is part of an eval\'s specification.',
 ['python3 labs/examples/gpt2_next_token.py']), ['hf-gpt2','hf-outputs'], 'tokenizer input_ids logits softmax topk hidden_states output_hidden_states pad attention_mask'),

lesson(13,3,'forward-hooks','Forward hooks: registering, capturing, and caching activations with shapes asserted',
 'A hook is a function the model calls for you after a module runs. Register it, copy what you need, assert the shape, and remove it. Every step has a way to go wrong.',
 '''<h2 id="the-api">The API in one paragraph</h2>
<p><code>module.register_forward_hook(hook)</code> registers a function with signature <code>hook(module, args, output) -&gt; None or modified output</code>, called every time after <code>forward()</code> has computed an output; it returns a handle, and <code>handle.remove()</code> unregisters it '''+cite('torch-module')+'''. The hook can return a modified output, which is how interventions are done; for caching you return nothing. <code>register_forward_pre_hook</code> runs before forward with the inputs, and <code>with_kwargs=True</code> passes keyword arguments too. That is the entire mechanism under TransformerLens's <code>HookPoint</code>s and most of nnsight.</p>
'''+run('python3 labs/examples/hooks_cache.py')+'''
<h2 id="read-the-script">Read the script</h2>
<p><code>make_hook(name)</code> is a closure factory from lesson 05: each call returns a hook that remembers its own name, so a list comprehension registers twelve distinct hooks in one line. Inside, <code>output[0] if isinstance(output, tuple) else output</code> normalises: in this transformers version a <code>GPT2Block</code> returns the residual-stream tensor directly, but other modules and other versions return tuples, and a hook that assumes one form breaks on the other. Then <code>.detach().clone()</code>, lessons 09 and 02: cut from the graph, and own the memory.</p>
<p>The forward pass runs inside <code>try</code> and the removal inside <code>finally</code>, so a failing forward cannot leave hooks behind. The output then checks every captured tensor against <code>(batch, seq, 768)</code>, <code>float32</code>, no grad, and finite. Those four asserts are the shape discipline of the course in four lines; put them in a function and call it after every capture.</p>
<h2 id="which-tensor">Which tensor did you capture?</h2>
<p>The two equality lines are the point of the script. <code>block.5</code> is identical to <code>hidden_states[6]</code>, confirming the convention from lesson 12. <code>block.11</code> is not identical to <code>hidden_states[12]</code>, because the tuple's last entry has <code>ln_f</code> applied and the block output does not. A hook on <code>transformer.drop</code> captures <code>hidden_states[0]</code>, the embedding output. With that mapping you can produce the whole <code>hidden_states</code> tuple from hooks, and the <code>cache_residual_stream</code> stub in <code>labs/hooks.py</code> asks you to do exactly that, with a test that checks the mapping entry by entry.</p>
<p>Hooks go anywhere in the tree, which is their advantage over <code>output_hidden_states</code>. <code>transformer.h.5.mlp</code> gives the MLP's contribution before it is added to the residual stream; <code>transformer.h.5.attn</code> gives attention's; <code>transformer.h.5.ln_1</code> gives what attention actually read. Those are the <code>hook_mlp_out</code>, <code>hook_attn_out</code> and <code>ln1.hook_normalized</code> of TransformerLens, and the table in lesson 14 maps them.</p>
<h2 id="to-disk">To disk and back</h2>
<p><code>torch.save(dict_of_tensors, path)</code> and <code>torch.load</code> round-trip the cache; 188 kB for twelve <code>(1, 5, 768)</code> float32 tensors, which is <code>12·5·768·4</code> bytes plus headers. <code>torch.load</code> uses pickle, so loading a file you did not produce executes whatever it contains; lesson 15 switches to safetensors for files that leave your machine. Either way, save the detached clones, never the raw outputs, and save a manifest beside the file that says which prompts, which blocks, and which convention.</p>
<h2 id="leaked-hook">The leaked hook</h2>
<p>The last lines register a hook, run a forward pass, and run another without removing it. The hook fires again. Nothing warns. In a notebook, where a cell is re-run ten times, that is ten hooks on one module, each appending to a list, and a cache that grows and slows with no error. The remedy is structural: register in a function that removes in <code>finally</code>, or use the context-manager idiom from lesson 05, and never register a hook at top level in a notebook. The lab test that counts hooks before and after your function is there to make the discipline mechanical.</p>
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In eval and interpretability work</span><p>The prediction-test habit from the roadmap starts here: before you look at an activation, write the assert that says what shape it must have and which block it came from. A cache that passes <code>assert_cache_shapes</code> is a cache you can make claims about; one that does not is a bug report.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>Storing <code>output</code> without <code>detach()</code> under a forward pass that was not in <code>no_grad</code>. The cache then holds the whole autograd graph of the forward pass, memory climbs with every prompt, and <code>torch.save</code> writes the graph's saved tensors too.</p></div>''',
 'A colleague\'s hook is <code>lambda m, a, o: cache.append(o)</code>, registered on every block, run over 100 prompts in a notebook. The cache has 1,200 entries the first time and 2,400 after re-running the cell. Explain both numbers and fix the hook in three changes.',
 '1,200 is 12 blocks times 100 prompts. 2,400 means the first set of hooks was never removed, so the re-run registered a second set and each block fired twice. Changes: store <code>o.detach().clone()</code> rather than <code>o</code>; key the cache by block and prompt rather than appending; and register inside a function that removes the handles in <code>finally</code>.',
 lab('Implement <code>cache_residual_stream</code> and <code>assert_cache_shapes</code> in <code>labs/hooks.py</code> and make <code>test_hooks.py</code> green. One test feeds an out-of-vocabulary id to make the forward pass fail; your hooks must still be gone afterwards.',
 'The embedding lookup raises on id 1,000,000, so the exception propagates through your function; <code>finally</code> runs; the hook counts match. The equality tests then confirm your mapping: <code>embed</code> from <code>transformer.drop</code>, <code>block.i</code> equal to <code>hidden_states[i + 1]</code> for i up to 10, and <code>block.11</code> differing from <code>hidden_states[12]</code> by <code>ln_f</code>.',
 ["python3 -m unittest discover -s labs -p 'test_hooks.py' 2>&1 | tail -1"]), ['torch-module','hf-outputs'], 'register_forward_hook handle remove closure detach clone cache torch.save finally'),

lesson(14,3,'three-toolkits','The same activation in transformers, TransformerLens 4, and nnsight',
 'Three toolkits, one residual stream. Two of them agree to the last bit; the third agrees only after you understand what its compatibility mode changed.',
 '''<h2 id="why-three">Why you need all three</h2>
<p>Papers and notebooks use whichever toolkit their authors used. TransformerLens gives named hook points and an <code>ActivationCache</code>; nnsight gives a tracing context that works on any Hugging Face model and can run remotely; raw hooks give you control and no dependency. You will read all three, and the readiness review asks you to reconcile them. The script below captures the residual stream after block 5 for the same prompt each way and compares the numbers.</p>
'''+run('python3 labs/examples/three_tools.py')+'''
<h2 id="transformerlens">TransformerLens 4: TransformerBridge</h2>
<p>TransformerLens 4.0 removed the legacy <code>HookedTransformer</code> stack; the replacement is <code>TransformerBridge.boot_transformers(name)</code>, optionally followed by <code>enable_compatibility_mode()</code> '''+cite('tl-v4')+'''. The bridge wraps the Hugging Face model and exposes TransformerLens names; <code>run_with_cache(prompt)</code> returns logits and a cache keyed by strings such as <code>blocks.5.hook_resid_post</code>, 493 entries for GPT-2 small. That key worked unchanged in the installed 4.2.0; the migration page does not promise it, so the transcript is the evidence.</p>
<p>Without compatibility mode the bridge uses the raw Hugging Face weights, and <code>hook_resid_post</code> matches the transformers hook to <code>2e-5</code>: float32 noise from a different order of operations. With compatibility mode the difference is 4.64, which looks like a bug and is not. Compatibility mode reproduces <code>HookedTransformer</code>'s default weight processing: LayerNorm folding, <code>center_writing_weights</code>, and <code>center_unembed</code> '''+cite('tl-v4')+'''. Centring the writing weights makes every vector written to the residual stream have zero mean over <code>d_model</code>, which is harmless to the model (LayerNorm removes the mean anyway) and changes every cached residual by exactly that mean. Subtract the mean over the last axis from the transformers activation, as the next line does, and the two agree to <code>5e-4</code>. <code>center_last_axis</code> in the shape lab is this operation.</p>
<p>The logits differ for the same reason: <code>center_unembed</code> subtracts the mean logit, which leaves the softmax and the argmax unchanged. And <code>prepend_bos</code> defaults to true, so the default call gives a sequence of 6, not 5; pass <code>prepend_bos=False</code> when comparing with other toolkits, and say which you used in your manifest.</p>
<h2 id="nnsight">nnsight: trace and save</h2>
<p><code>LanguageModel("gpt2")</code> wraps the model, and the module tree keeps Hugging Face's names. Inside <code>with model.trace(prompt):</code>, <code>model.transformer.h[5].output</code> refers to that module's forward-pass return value, and <code>.save()</code> marks it to be returned after the trace '''+cite('nnsight-getting')+'''. The body of the block is captured and run through the backend rather than executed inline '''+cite('nnsight-tracer')+'''; saved values are real tensors once the block exits, which is why the <code>print</code> is after the <code>with</code>. The numbers are bit-identical to the raw hook, because nnsight is running the same model with no weight processing.</p>
<p>Two behaviours follow from "captured, not executed". Access modules in forward order inside one trace: saving <code>wte.output</code> after <code>h[5].output</code> raises an out-of-order error, because the model has already run past the embedding when the request is made. And a <code>trace</code> block must live in a file or a notebook cell; typed into an interactive <code>python3</code> prompt it fails, because the tracer needs the source of the block. The roadmap recorded both when verifying the toolchain.</p>
<h2 id="name-map">One table for three names</h2>
<table class="namemap"><tr><th>What</th><th>transformers hook on</th><th>TransformerLens 4 cache key</th><th>nnsight</th></tr>
<tr><td>embedding output</td><td><code>transformer.drop</code></td><td><code>blocks.0.hook_resid_pre</code></td><td><code>model.transformer.drop.output</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>residual after block i</td><td><code>transformer.h[i]</code></td><td><code>blocks.{i}.hook_resid_post</code></td><td><code>model.transformer.h[i].output</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>residual between attn and mlp</td><td>pre-hook on <code>transformer.h[i].ln_2</code></td><td><code>blocks.{i}.hook_resid_mid</code></td><td><code>model.transformer.h[i].ln_2.input</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>attention output (before residual add)</td><td><code>transformer.h[i].attn</code> (returns a tuple)</td><td><code>blocks.{i}.hook_attn_out</code></td><td><code>model.transformer.h[i].attn.output[0]</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>MLP output</td><td><code>transformer.h[i].mlp</code></td><td><code>blocks.{i}.hook_mlp_out</code></td><td><code>model.transformer.h[i].mlp.output</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>attention pattern (b, h, q, k)</td><td><code>model(..., output_attentions=True).attentions[i]</code></td><td><code>blocks.{i}.attn.hook_pattern</code></td><td>not a module output; use the transformers argument</td></tr>
<tr><td>final LayerNorm output</td><td><code>transformer.ln_f</code></td><td><code>ln_final.hook_normalized</code></td><td><code>model.transformer.ln_f.output</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>logits (b, s, vocab)</td><td><code>model(...).logits</code></td><td>first return of <code>run_with_cache</code></td><td><code>model.output.logits</code></td></tr></table>
<p>The table is checked, not recalled. The second script reads five of the rows through nnsight and compares each with the raw bridge's cache key; all agree to float32 noise, and it shows that the attention module returns a tuple whose first element is the output. The TransformerLens keys are from the 493 the bridge produced for GPT-2 small. Return types do change between transformers versions, so assert the shape before trusting any row on a new install.</p>
'''+run('python3 labs/examples/name_map_check.py')+'''
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In eval and interpretability work</span><p>When a notebook's numbers do not match yours, the first three questions are: was a BOS token prepended, was compatibility mode on, and which layer convention was used. All three change the tensor without changing the model, and all three belong in a manifest.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>Comparing a TransformerLens cache in compatibility mode with raw hidden states and concluding the model is "different". It is the same model with centred writing weights. Centre the raw activation over <code>d_model</code> before comparing, or load the bridge without compatibility mode when you want raw numbers.</p></div>''',
 'A TransformerLens notebook reports that the residual stream at <code>blocks.5.hook_resid_post</code> has a mean of exactly zero across features at every position. Your transformers hook gives a mean of about 0.07. Which is "right", and what claim would be wrong to make?',
 'Both are right for what they are. The bridge in compatibility mode centred the writing weights, so a zero feature-mean is a property of its representation, not of GPT-2. A claim such as "GPT-2\'s residual stream is centred" is wrong; "after centring, these two directions are orthogonal" is fine if you say you centred. Record the mode.',
 lab('Run <code>three_tools.py</code>, then change <code>LAYER</code> to 11 and run again. Predict first whether the nnsight and transformers numbers will still be identical and whether the compatibility-mode difference will grow.',
 'Identical still, for nnsight; the mechanism does not depend on the layer. The compatibility-mode difference grows with the residual norm, which lesson 12 showed climbing to several hundred by block 11, and still vanishes after centring. If you also compare <code>ln_final.hook_normalized</code> with <code>hidden_states[12]</code>, expect a scale difference from folded LayerNorm weights.',
 ['python3 labs/examples/three_tools.py']), ['tl-v4','tl-releases','nnsight-getting','nnsight-tracer'], 'TransformerLens TransformerBridge boot_transformers compatibility mode run_with_cache nnsight trace save prepend_bos'),

lesson(15,3,'reproducibility-and-artifacts','Reproducibility and artifacts: seeds, safetensors, a run manifest, memory and batching on CPU',
 'An activation file is a claim: these prompts, this model, this convention produced these numbers. The manifest is what lets someone else check it.',
 '''<h2 id="seeds">Seeds, and what they do not promise</h2>
<p><code>torch.manual_seed(n)</code> seeds the generator for all devices '''+cite('torch-random')+'''; the script shows two <code>randn</code> calls after the same seed producing the same numbers. NumPy has its own generator, seeded separately, and <code>np.random.default_rng(n)</code> is the modern form. Seed both at the top of anything that samples. What seeds do not promise is agreement across machines: the documentation says completely reproducible results are not guaranteed across PyTorch releases, individual commits, or platforms '''+cite('torch-random')+'''. On one machine with one version, the output shows a repeated GPT-2 forward pass is bit-identical; across machines expect float32 noise, and write your comparisons with <code>allclose</code> and a tolerance you state.</p>
'''+run('python3 labs/examples/reproducibility.py')+'''
<h2 id="memory">Memory arithmetic you should do in your head</h2>
<p>A float32 is 4 bytes. Thirteen hidden states of <code>(2, 5, 768)</code> is <code>13·2·5·768·4</code>, 0.40 MB, matching the output. The same stack for a 1024-token prompt is 41 MB; a batch of 32 of them is 1.3 GB, which is where a laptop starts swapping. The rules that follow: cache only the layers you need, not all thirteen; keep <code>no_grad</code> on so no graph is held; process prompts in batches and write each batch to disk before the next; and store in float16 only when you have checked the precision loss is acceptable for the comparison you will make. Model weights are 124M parameters times 4 bytes, about 500 MB, loaded once.</p>
<p>Batching trades memory for speed and introduces padding. Pad with the EOS token, pass the attention mask, and record the mask in the manifest, as the script does, so that whoever reads the file knows which positions are real. Prompts of very different lengths waste compute on padding; sort by length and batch neighbours.</p>
<h2 id="safetensors">safetensors over pickle</h2>
<p><code>torch.save</code> pickles, and pickle executes code on load. The safetensors format stores tensors safely, as opposed to pickle, and is still fast with zero-copy loading '''+cite('safetensors')+'''; <code>save_file(dict_of_tensors, path)</code> and <code>load_file</code> are the whole API for the common case. It requires contiguous tensors, hence the <code>.contiguous()</code> calls, and it is the format the <code>gpt2</code> checkpoint itself uses in your cache. The 0.40 MB file round-trips with <code>torch.equal</code> true. Use it for anything you share; use <code>torch.save</code> for a scratch file you will delete.</p>
<h2 id="manifest">The run manifest</h2>
<p>A manifest is a small JSON file beside the tensors answering the questions lesson 14 raised. The script writes six keys: the model name, the prompts in order, the seed, the library versions, the attention mask, and for every tensor its shape, dtype and a checksum of its bytes. With sorted keys and no timestamps, the same run produces the same file, which means <code>diff</code> can tell you whether two runs agree before you load a single tensor. The <code>manifest.py</code> lab asks for this as four functions, with tests that check the checksum is stable across a non-contiguous view and that every kind of mismatch is reported rather than raised.</p>
<p>Add to that list anything that changed the numbers in lesson 14: whether a BOS token was prepended, whether compatibility mode was on, and which layer convention the keys use. A manifest that says <code>block.5</code> means "output of transformer.h.5, before ln_f" has answered the off-by-one question for everyone downstream.</p>
<h2 id="artifact-habits">Habits that make artifacts checkable</h2>
<p>Name files by content, not by date: <code>gpt2_resid_post_blocks0-11_fp32.safetensors</code>. Keep the script that produced an artifact next to it, and make the script idempotent so re-running it overwrites rather than appends. Put a <code>check_manifest</code> call at the start of any script that consumes the file, so a stale or truncated file fails fast. And record the command line and the environment, which is what <code>scripts/verify_commands.py</code> does for this course's own outputs: the transcript at the top of each lesson's output is the same discipline applied to the course itself.</p>
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In eval and interpretability work</span><p>Every claim in the interpretability course will be of the form "on these activations, this prediction held". The activations are an artifact with a manifest, or the claim cannot be checked by anyone, including you in three weeks.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>Saving activations in float16 to halve the file and then testing a direction with a tolerance of <code>1e-5</code>. float16 has about three significant digits; the comparison is meaningless. Decide the precision from the test you will run, and write it in the manifest.</p></div>''',
 'Two manifests from two machines agree on every shape and dtype and disagree on every checksum. The argmax tokens agree. What do you conclude and what do you do?',
 'Float32 non-determinism across platforms, which the reproducibility notes say to expect: the bytes differ at the last digits and the checksums are hashes of bytes. Confirm by loading both files and checking <code>allclose</code> with a stated tolerance, then record that tolerance and both version strings in a comparison note. Do not "fix" it by regenerating until the hashes match; they will not.',
 lab('Implement <code>labs/manifest.py</code> and make its tests green. Then extend a copy of <code>reproducibility.py</code> to call your <code>build_manifest</code> and <code>check_manifest</code> on the safetensors round trip, and deliberately corrupt one value before checking.',
 'The digest test with a transposed view forces <code>np.ascontiguousarray</code> before hashing. <code>check_manifest</code> must return strings, not raise, so a consumer can report all problems at once. A single changed value changes the digest, and your check names the tensor.',
 ["python3 -m unittest discover -s labs -p 'test_manifest.py' 2>&1 | tail -1"]), ['torch-random','safetensors'], 'seed manual_seed determinism safetensors save_file manifest sha256 memory batching padding float16'),
]
